From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 04:04:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(buildcheck): read per-test results out of a check's
 output

Ninth slice: "the check failed" is not actionable, the report has to name the
test. TaskReport previously carried only the class of failure.

core/buildcheck/TestReport.kt parses three shapes out of the output the app
already holds, strongest evidence first:
- JUnit XML (what Gradle writes under build/test-results): totals from the
  testsuite attributes, status from each testcase. A hand-written scanner
  rather than an XML library, because the input is the tail of a log and may be
  cut mid-tag, where a real parser throws and this reports what it could read.
- Gradle's per-test console lines, with the first indented line of the trace as
  the message.
- A summary line: real counts, no names.

The numbers are kept honest rather than optimistic:
- an unrecognised log is TestReportSource.NONE with total 0, so a green exit
  code with no report never becomes "all tests passed";
- when the declared totals and the enumerated cases disagree (truncated log, or
  a run that only printed failures) countsDisagree is set and the report says
  so instead of showing a confident wrong number;
- names and messages are flattened, bounded (200/300 chars) and redacted, so a
  stack trace or a captured token never reaches the report;
- the scan itself is bounded (512 KiB, 5000 cases, 50 listed failures) because
  the text comes from a subprocess.

TaskReport.testSummaries() puts the result in the end-of-task report under a
"tests:" section, and renders nothing when no check produced a report.

Tests: TestReportParserTest (18), plus two TaskReportBuilderTest cases (15).
