 `output` itself is truncated to the command's `maxOutputBytes`.
 
+## Per-test reports
+
+"the check failed" is not actionable - the report has to name the test. `TestReportParser` reads three
+shapes out of the output the app already holds, strongest first:
+
+1. **JUnit XML** (what Gradle writes to `build/test-results/…`): totals from the `testsuite` attributes,
+   statuses from each `testcase`. A hand-written scanner, not an XML library, because the input is the tail
+   of a log and may be cut mid-tag; a real parser would throw where this reports what it could read.
+2. **Gradle's per-test console lines** (`com.example.FooTest > name FAILED`), with the first indented line of
+   the trace as the message.
+3. **A summary line** (`12 tests completed, 2 failed`) - real counts, no names.
+
+Rules that keep the numbers honest: an unrecognised log yields `TestReportSource.NONE` and `total = 0`, never
+"all tests passed" (`isEmpty`); when the declared totals and the enumerated cases disagree (a truncated log,
+or a run that only printed failures) `countsDisagree` is set and the report says so; test names and messages
+are flattened, bounded (200 / 300 characters) and passed through `SecretRedaction`; the scan is bounded
+(512 KiB, 5,000 cases, 50 listed failures). `CheckResult.testReport()` reads a finished check, and
+`TaskReport.testSummaries()` puts the result in the end-of-task report. Nothing is executed: this parses text.
+
